adrian nell was in mozambique building  an accommodation block with his father  and younger brother  but he was killed on friday as they  tried to make their escape from a hotel  under attack by suspected islamist  insurgents  news of his death reached the rest of  his family in south africa  a day later my son is very traumatized  as is i'm sure my husband but he's being  very strong  and luckily they were able to  bring adrian's body back to camber with  them  so they're just waiting for that  all the paperwork to be done so we can  get them back to south africa  nearly 200 civilians were sheltering  inside the hotel in the town of palmer  after militants surrounded the compound  helicopters could be heard circling  above  but a convoy of cars was deemed the only  route out  in total seven were killed when it was  ambushed and dozens more  in the town itself  the defense and security forces have  regretfully recorded the loss of life of  a group of citizens  who rushed into a column of vehicles  leaving from the amarula hotel  and were ambushed by terrorists we  regret this loss of life  and inform you that we are continuing  our work to eliminate the terrorists  the attack was part of a wider conflict  in the predominantly muslim region of  cabo delgado  palmers in the far north close to gas  projects worth more than  40 billion pounds the group of civilians  both locals and expats were trapped  inside the amarula hotel  those who survived have been taken to  pember further south  but many are still missing including  this woman's brother  you don't know where the person is you  can't reach them in what condition  they're in you know  this is horrible it's destabilizing it's  really not fair  the insurgency in mozambique began in  2017  through a group known as ansar al-sana  more than two and a half thousand people  have died in the fighting  and seven hundred thousand have been  displaced  experts hope the events of this week  will prompt action from authorities  in the short term flooding the area  with government security forces and  providing outposts and patrols on an  ongoing basis  is probably the only immediate response  but there needs to be a think of how  locals can benefit from this  from the economic expansion that is  happening in the area  for now though the only focus of those  caught up in this conflict  is survival iva bennett sky news
 

